[[:db/retract 17592186198560 :question/answer-fields 17592186193991] {:db/id 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198560 :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" "answer-5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" "answer-f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" "answer-5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" "answer-7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" :question/id #uuid "5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138128" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb" :answer-field/id #uuid "62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" "answer-1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" "answer-56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" "answer-8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" "answer-6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" :answer/id #uuid "5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" :answer/id #uuid "1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" :answer/id #uuid "56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" :answer/id #uuid "8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer/id #uuid "6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f0bc9465-a035-5136-b09d-b009ddf817a7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0bc9465-a035-5136-b09d-b009ddf817a7" :question/id #uuid "f0bc9465-a035-5136-b09d-b009ddf817a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170400" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-59381183-ff09-50a9-9c38-82023491a255"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-59381183-ff09-50a9-9c38-82023491a255" :answer-field/id #uuid "59381183-ff09-50a9-9c38-82023491a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa1e17c0-a680-5fd1-840a-1550ea0d3fa6" "answer-0403e816-9a8a-5caa-a60f-9e0db37c2581" "answer-2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" "answer-c87e6493-0b51-5dfe-8402-a4a84620be0b" "answer-9cbc00fe-d9dc-5e75-8939-93c74926f323"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa1e17c0-a680-5fd1-840a-1550ea0d3fa6" :answer/id #uuid "fa1e17c0-a680-5fd1-840a-1550ea0d3fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0403e816-9a8a-5caa-a60f-9e0db37c2581" :answer/id #uuid "0403e816-9a8a-5caa-a60f-9e0db37c2581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" :answer/id #uuid "2ad356f6-e2a2-578b-805e-5d220905af93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c87e6493-0b51-5dfe-8402-a4a84620be0b" :answer/id #uuid "c87e6493-0b51-5dfe-8402-a4a84620be0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cbc00fe-d9dc-5e75-8939-93c74926f323" :answer/id #uuid "9cbc00fe-d9dc-5e75-8939-93c74926f323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" "question-dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" :question/id #uuid "3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170506" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" "answer-5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" "answer-a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" "answer-1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" "answer-31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" :answer/id #uuid "1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" :answer/id #uuid "5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" :answer/id #uuid "a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer/id #uuid "1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056" :answer/id #uuid "31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/id #uuid "dea55e7b-1cc0-500b-8640-ae39dddce75c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170560" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $25.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b0d1a4-b3d8-5bd2-80e4-83dea5aa94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" "answer-27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" "answer-b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" "answer-a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" "answer-566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/id #uuid "3bb153c6-9455-5e09-b9d4-e4c936682c6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/id #uuid "27fa6c83-fea5-559c-b3af-e6a7e1618346" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/id #uuid "b119d151-632a-5e08-8a6e-94f8c7ba763c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/id #uuid "a93b100e-c392-5adc-a3ed-bee963bcaac6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/id #uuid "566bcdea-96b1-557a-bdcd-81a53378a8b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f2727b60-7a6b-5f3a-a237-6a67bfb8139d" :activity/id #uuid "f2727b60-7a6b-5f3a-a237-6a67bfb8139d" :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048933 :activity/course 17592186046673 :activity/steps ["step-cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" "step-0d7e6a3a-4e90-51f7-9e6d-1f36e15dea08" "step-f08afe40-e4fb-52cb-a8c1-acffe9c0cb58" "step-80ab5b9e-fd44-5cd4-b555-7638c14ed974" "step-3781c4aa-7e7b-5b51-9af4-d354711b0091"] :activity/first-step "step-cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" :step/id #uuid "cd806b8f-e6cf-5b59-a4ed-ccc0cfee81a2" :step/content 17592186198560 :step/next "s